XXXV. THE THREE COMPETING HYPOTHESES
H1 — PSYCHOLOGICAL / ENACTIVE

[
\text{Imaginal practice}
\rightarrow
\text{identity}
\rightarrow
\text{salience}
\rightarrow
\text{motivation}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{changed outcome}
]

No paranormal mechanism required.

This alone could be extraordinarily valuable.

H2 — INEXTERNALIST

Intrinsic phenomenal organization and extrinsic world-involving behavior are aspects of one unfolding process.

Changing the configuration of the aperture is therefore not merely changing an isolated “inner representation.”

It is changing one side of an already world-involving dynamic.

[
\Delta \text{intrinsic organization}
\leftrightarrow
\Delta \text{world trajectory}
]

This does not require supernatural intervention.

But it rejects the assumption that inner and outer were metaphysically independent domains in the first place.

H3 — STRONG PARTICIPATORY MANIFESTATION

Intentional configuration of consciousness contributes to probability/outcome selection in ways not exhausted by H1/H2's ordinary behavioral and relational pathways.

Kepple's Focus-2 probability-selection account is an explicit version of something like H3.

H3 requires the strongest evidence.

Do not infer it merely because the target succeeds.

XXXVI. THE MOST IMPORTANT CONTROL

Suppose the exact target occurs.

That alone does not establish H3.

You may simply have spent eight years:

obsessively clarifying it;
acquiring the correct skills;
noticing opportunities;
making money;
meeting the right people;
moving toward the correct geography;
building habits;
refusing irrelevant alternatives.

That would be a spectacular validation of the practice while remaining compatible with H1.

The strong thesis requires something harder.

That distinction must survive success.

XXXVII. THE ABHINAVAGUPTA CHECKPOINT

Your IPVV project already solved the most dangerous conceptual problem.

The argument proceeds from felt reflexivity toward deeper identity—but its own knowledge core says the final universalization remains the commitment, not an automatic entailment of the earlier arguments.

Therefore:

Recognition Level 1

There is self-luminous/reflexive experience.

Recognition Level 2

The ordinary self-model does not exhaust what I am.

Recognition Level 3

Localization need not entail ontological isolation.

Recognition Level 4 — Śaiva wager

The ground of this awareness is universal Śiva-consciousness.

Practice may inhabit Level 4 completely.

Research must remember that Level 4 remains the metaphysical wager.

That is exactly how to take it seriously.

XXXVIII. WHAT “I AM ŚIVA” MEANS IN THE PROTOCOL

It does not mean:

My preferences are cosmic commands.

It means:

I refuse to identify the source of consciousness solely with the contracted autobiographical representation appearing inside consciousness.

It further proposes:

The capacity to illuminate, know, intend and act appearing here participates in the very structure by which reality becomes determinate.

The practical consequence is not grandiosity.

It is responsibility.

If you are going to claim creative power, then there is less excuse for:

passivity;
chaos;
learned helplessness;
endlessly contradictory desires;
refusing feedback;
blaming reality while taking no action.

Śiva acts.

XXXIX. NO ENTITY IS REQUIRED

The complete system works without:

angels;
guides;
daimons;
extraterrestrials;
channeled beings.

Their materials are comparative technologies.

Recognition remains sufficient.

If autonomous intelligences are encountered, they become additional research objects.

They never become necessary metaphysical middlemen between the experimenter and consciousness.

XL. THE MASTER FORMULA

Reduce the entire protocol to this:

RECOGNIZE

I am not merely the contracted self-positioning.

CHOOSE

Specify the world.

INHABIT

Experience it until it possesses phenomenological depth.

TASTE

Find its rasa.

LISTEN

Allow unexpected material to emerge.

KNOW

Extract information about the bridge.

ACT

Apply the correct force through the correct medium to the correct object.

RELEASE

Do not demand the route.

OBSERVE

Record what reality actually does.

CORRECT

Change the map, not the evidence.

REPEAT

For years.

XLI. THE MINIMUM VIABLE DAY

If everything falls apart, preserve four things:

3 minutes Recognition

10 minutes end-state bhāvanā

one meaningful kriyā action

2 minutes logging

Never allow perfectionism to produce a zero.

XLII. THE FAILED DAY

Never compensate.

Never perform three hours of visualization because yesterday was missed.

Record:

MISSED.

Resume.

A clean dataset is more valuable than a guilt ritual.

